WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.400
SPEAKER: You can use
custom dashboards

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.510
to tailor the monitoring
experience to your environment.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.760
To create your first
custom dashboard,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.850
click Dashboards at the top
of the screen and Create.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:16.450
Here, we'll add three
charts to our new dashboard.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.810
First, I'll choose a resource
type of Google App Engine

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.380
Module.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:27.060
And then I'll search
for latency metrics.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:32.200
Here, I'll add the response
latency's 95th percentile.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.730
So now I have a chart that
displays the 95th percentile

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.720
latency for each of
my App Engine modules.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.550
I'll add a second chart for
the App Engine responses

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:45.880
for each of the modules.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:50.590
So again, I'll type
Responses to filter the list.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:55.830
And then I'll choose Total
Responses and click Save.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.460
And I'll add a third chart
for Google Compute Engine

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.930
instances.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:05.099
In this case, I'm primarily
interested in CPU usage

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:07.410
for each of my instances.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.380
And I'll click Save.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.890
So now I have three charts
on my custom dashboard.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.710
I can also change the
layout of that dashboard.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:20.210
Perhaps I want to view it
in a three-column view.

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:23.000
I can also rearrange the
charts on the dashboard

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.110
by clicking on any of
the titles and moving it

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:30.080
into its location.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.140
If I want to look at the
dashboard in full screen mode,

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:36.350
I can click the Full Screen icon
in the top right-hand corner

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:37.830
of the screen.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:41.060
This is particularly useful
for viewing the dashboard

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.790
on a large LED
screen in an office.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:48.170
To the left of the
Full Screen icon,

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.720
you can access the
settings for the chart,

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.030
such as the setting to show all
of the legends on the charts.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:59.830
And to the left of that, I can
choose to enable auto-refresh

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:00.535
for my charts.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.600
And then if I've zoomed
in any of the charts

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.199
to a particular time
frame, I can always

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:12.380
use the Reset Zoom
icon to reset the time

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.440
frame across all of my charts.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.980
And then finally, if I wanted
to choose a specific time

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.080
frame for the
charts, I can click

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.750
on any of the links in
the top right-hand corner

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:26.390
to view that time frame.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:29.000
Here, I'm looking at
a day's worth of data

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.890
across all of my charts.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.410
And so those are the core
capabilities of the Custom

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.300
Dashboard feature.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.160
There are many more
capabilities for you

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.340
to take advantage of as
you familiarize yourself

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.920
with custom dashboards.

